<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arabian Peninsula</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Arabian Peninsula is the large peninsula of south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>. It was the base from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> launched the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Islamic conquests</a>. It was also connected through trade to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire</a> across the Red Sea and to the Babylonian world, and was visited by Ming Chinese fleets.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula.jpg" alt="Arabian Peninsula" width="320" height="292" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SeaWiFS Project, NASA/Goddard Space Flight Center, and ORBIMAGE · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arabian_Peninsula_dust_SeaWiFS.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Arabian Peninsula, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, covers about 3,237,500 square kilometres and is the world's largest peninsula. It is bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> in the west, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aden">Gulf of Aden</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> in the south, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-oman">Gulf of Oman</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> in the east, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> in the north. Most of it is covered by the Arabian Desert, and mountains rise in the south-west, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> receives more rain. Few permanent rivers exist, but where ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquifer">aquifers</a> surface oases form, and palm groves made the peninsula a great producer of dates.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A finger bone found at Al Wusta in the Nefud Desert, about 90,000 years old, is the oldest human fossil found outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. The written record is thought to begin in the early 1st millennium BCE. South Arabia saw the Kingdom of Saba, Awsan, Main and Himyar, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> the Kingdom of Kinda, and eastern Arabia the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilmun">Dilmun</a> civilization. From 106 to 630 CE the north-west was under Roman control as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia-petraea">Arabia Petraea</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a> as its capital. Greco-Roman writers also spoke of Arabia Deserta for the centre and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia-felix">Arabia Felix</a> for the south.</p><p>Muhammad was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> about 570, began preaching there in 610 and migrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> in 622. After his death in 632 Abu Bakr became the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> and put down the Ridda wars, a rebellion of Arab tribes. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> and, from 661, the Umayyad caliphs, Arab armies conquered lands from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and the political focus of the Muslim world shifted away from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, though Mecca and Medina remained its spiritual centres and pilgrimage destinations. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> never controlled central Arabia, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-saud">Al Saud</a> began to emerge in 1744, when Muhammad bin Saud joined the religious leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-abd-al-wahhab">Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diriyah">Diriyah</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 1803. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-revolt">Arab Revolt</a> of 1916 to 1918, begun by Sherif Hussein ibn Ali, challenged Ottoman rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a> in 1902 and by 1926 had subdued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">Al-Hasa</a>, Jabal Shammar and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>. Vast oil reserves were discovered in the 1930s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Nine countries lie wholly or partly on the peninsula: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwait">Kuwait</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatar">Qatar</a>, Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, with the southern halves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and Jordan. Saudi Arabia covers the largest part. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a> and the Prophet's Mosque, remain the principal destinations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arabian Peninsula” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Prophet Muhammad died in Medina in June 632 CE without a universally acknowledged successor, and the uncertainty threatened to dissolve the young Muslim community before it had consolidated control over the <b>Arabian Peninsula</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Unification in 1990 realised a goal that both Yemeni states had professed for decades, and for its first years the republic was the most open political system on the <b>Arabian Peninsula</b>, with competitive elections, opposition parties, women voting and standing for parliament, and a relatively free press.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Republic of Yemen · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…struggle against British colonial rule made the NLF a symbol for radical movements across the Arab world, and its support for the Dhofar rebels and other leftist groups made it a factor in the politics of the <b>Arabian Peninsula</b> and the Horn of Africa during the Cold War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-yemen">South Yemen · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The rare lasting unions are the forcible unification of much of the <b>Arabian Peninsula</b> by the Saudi rulers of Najd in the 1920s, the United Arab Emirates and the unification of North and South Yemen.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pan-arabism">Pan-Arabism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>The civil service, headed by the Gyaasehene, drew men talented in trade, diplomacy and war, and the king appointed them all, including men from the <b>Arabian Peninsula</b>, Sudan and Europe.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ashanti">Social Hierarchy of the Ashanti Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>The first of two major Near Eastern earthquakes that year had its epicentre in the northwestern <b>Arabian Peninsula</b>, around Tabuk.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-18">18 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
